ension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) indicating major haemodynamic instability (shock). Severe localized right upper quadrant pain with diffuse tenderness suggests potential intra-abdominal source requiring immediate stabilization.", "required_actions": ["Continue IV fluid resuscitation", "Monitor for response to fluids", "Consider vasopressor support if hypotension persists", "Order lactate level and CBC to assess shock severity", "Prepare for emergent imaging (FAST exam or CT) if hemodynamic stability achieved"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch meets posterior >=0.75 confirmation threshold. Dangerous alternatives (B1.2: PID, B1.3: Ovarian torsion) remain unresolved with posterior >=0.10. Emergency override requires immediate stabilization (IV fluids), but further diagnostic clarification is needed to determine specific management."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2857142857142857, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'acute pancreatitis', 'gas', 'epigastric pain'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.476}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic / Hepatobiliary Vascular Source of Shock", "posterior": 0.286, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Absence of hepatomegaly/ascites weakly argues against hepatic/vascular shock explanations like Budd-Chiari syndrome."}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.2", "B3", "B1.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=both]\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Shock vs Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Septic Shock:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, jaundice, loss of appetite\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nBiliary Obstruction with Shock vs Ovarian Torsion with Hemorrhagic Shock:\n  Favours first:  clay-colored stools, dark urine, fatigue, fever, jaundice\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, altered mental status, anxiety\n\nPelvic Inflammatory Disease with Septic Shock vs Ovarian Torsion with Hemorrhagic Shock:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, altered mental status, anxiety\n\nRelated to Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Septic Shock: abdominal and pelvic region disorder, acute female pelvic peritonitis, female reproductive system disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Septic Shock\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Septic Shock\n  The pain has been dull and progressive → arthralgia → Pelvic Inflammatory Disease with Septic Shock"}
